-- title:
--   Dyadic budgets of candidate ordinals
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$.
--
--   1. **Candidate budget.** The candidate with zero-based ordinal $n$ has budget
--   $$
--   \beta(n)=2^{-(n+9)} .
--   $$
--   2. **Nonroot nodes.** Pairs $(k,v)$ with $v$ a node of level $k+1$.
--   3. **Retained budget.** A retained node inherits the budget of its global candidate label. Since labels are injective, the total retained budget is at most $\sum_n2^{-(n+9)}=2^{-8}$.
